Acromegaly
Endocrine & Metabolic Disorders
Enlarged hands/feet, Coarse facial features, Prognathism/Macroglossia, Elevated IGF-1

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-001
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe headache with new-onset visual blurring and debilitating large joint arthropathy causing severe mobility impairment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated sudden worsening of headache, progressive visual field changes, and profound inability to mobilize. Immediate, urgent physical and neurological assessment was required out-of-hours to exclude impending pituitary apoplexy or critical optic chiasm compression, preventing severe neurological deterioration before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head is throbbing so badly I can't see straight out of the sides of my eyes, and my hands are too swollen and painful to even hold a cup."
History of Present Illness: Over the past 48 hours, the patient reports a crescendo pattern of severe, unrelenting bilateral headache, complicated today by a subjective loss of peripheral vision. This is superimposed on a chronic, insidious progressive enlargement of the hands and feet necessitating larger ring and shoe sizes over the past two years. The patient also notes coarsening of facial features with prominent supraorbital ridges and frontal bossing. Symptoms are compounded by profound proximal muscle weakness and severe large joint arthropathy, rendering the patient unable to walk safely to the bathroom tonight. Additional reported symptoms include hyperhidrosis, increased skin oiliness, and deepening of the voice due to vocal cord hypertrophy.
Functional Status: Usually independent, currently requiring assistance to mobilize safely due to joint pain and visual deficit.
Social/Safety Concerns: Fall risk secondary to visual impairment and severe arthropathy. Spouse expresses high distress regarding the rapid decline in the patient's mobility.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/92 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98 percent on room air | Wt: 105 Kg
General: Distressed by headache and joint pain. Appears diaphoretic with notably coarse facial features.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals a partial bitemporal hemianopsia on confrontation testing. Musculoskeletal exam demonstrates enlarged, doughy hands and feet with restricted, painful range of motion in bilateral shoulders and knees. Head and neck exam exhibits marked prognathism resulting in dental malocclusion and widened tooth spaces, alongside macroglossia. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are unremarkable. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: Enlarged hands/feet, Coarse facial features, Prognathism/Macroglossia, and anticipated Elevated IGF-1 pending future laboratory correlation.
Environmental Observations: Clear pathways, but dim lighting in the hallway presents a trip hazard for a patient with new visual deficits. Temperature adequately controlled.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol and ibuprofen checked against recent pharmacy dispensing records. No discrepancies noted.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acromegaly with suspected macroadenoma expansion - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Migraine with aura - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of fixed bitemporal visual field deficits, lack of classical scintillating scotoma, and the prominent somatic changes.

Rheumatoid arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of classic small joint symmetrical polyarthritis and the lack of morning stiffness exceeding one hour, combined with profound facial and acral morphological changes.

Primary Obstructive Sleep Apnoea - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct acute onset of severe headache and visual field defects driving the presentation tonight, despite the presence of macroglossia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of a worsening, severe headache coupled with new-onset bitemporal hemianopsia and debilitating arthropathy represents a high-risk scenario warranting immediate evaluation. The telephone triage correctly identified the potential for critical optic chiasm compression or pituitary apoplexy. The objective findings of fixed peripheral visual loss and acute functional decline confirm that this assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period. Urgent after-hours intervention was essential to determine the immediate need for neurosurgical or endocrine escalation, satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours MBS billing.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral analgesia (paracetamol 1g, ibuprofen 400mg) for joint pain and headache. Recommended urgent comprehensive laboratory workup including serum IGF-1, oral glucose tolerance test for growth hormone suppression, and comprehensive anterior pituitary hormone profile at the earliest in-hours opportunity.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you use a bright, focused nightlight in the hallway (action), you will clearly see the floor edges (cause), which will directly prevent you from tripping during nighttime bathroom visits (effect). By deliberately pacing your movements when rising from a chair (action), you allow your joints time to adjust to the load (cause), reducing sudden sharp pain and preventing falls (effect).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral for community physiotherapy for joint mobility preservation.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provision of a stable walking frame for nighttime mobility.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24 hours to coordinate urgent MRI pituitary and endocrinology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the nearest Emergency Department or call 000 if the headache becomes sudden and thunderclap in nature, if there is a rapid or complete loss of vision, intractable vomiting, or an altered level of consciousness.

